# Minimal base. Installed with --apt-recommends false, so every package here is deliberate.
# Remote access, discovery, printing, Bluetooth and VM desktop-integration (clipboard / drag-and-drop /
# shared folders) packages are intentionally absent; tests/security/test_os_hardening.py enforces this.

# Boot and system
linux-image-amd64
live-boot
systemd-sysv
systemd-resolved
udev
dbus
ca-certificates

# Security
nftables
apparmor
apparmor-utils
cryptsetup
cryptsetup-initramfs

# Hypervisor guest services without desktop integration (time sync, graceful shutdown)
open-vm-tools

# Runtime for the backend (Python version follows the Debian release; requirement is >= 3.12)
python3
python3-venv
sqlite3

# Python libraries the backend imports (backend/, installed at /opt/pai/backend by os/build/assemble-config.sh).
# Debian's own packages, not pip: signed and security-updated by the archive and frozen with it by PAI_SNAPSHOT
# (threat T12), with nothing to compile or download on the image. apt pulls their hard dependencies too (anyio, h11,
# click, wsproto, uvloop, ...), tested on trixie with --no-install-recommends. tests/security/test_image_assembly.py
# fails when the backend imports a third-party module that has no python3-<module> package here.
# fastapi: the HTTP application (backend/api).
python3-fastapi
# starlette: imported directly (the request guard middleware, the threadpool helper); fastapi is built on it.
python3-starlette
# uvicorn: the ASGI server that python -m backend runs.
python3-uvicorn
# pydantic: the settings model and the API response models.
python3-pydantic

# Kiosk shell (os/kiosk, kiosk/): the UI runs in Chromium under cage on tty1. Installed with --apt-recommends false.
# Sizes: docs/development/KIOSK.md. cage and Chromium pull in a lot as hard dependencies (wlroots, Mesa and its software
# renderer, Xwayland, GTK 3, Mesa's LLVM): none of it is listed here, none can be left out, and none starts a service.
# cage: the Wayland compositor that shows one application full screen (the launcher). It takes the display and input
# devices from logind, draws with wlroots' software renderer in a virtual machine without a GPU, and has no panel,
# menu or way to leave the application.
cage
# chromium: the browser, Debian's own build (signed and security-updated by the archive, frozen with it by
# PAI_SNAPSHOT: threat T12). Its sandbox needs unprivileged user namespaces, which Debian kernels allow, so the
# setuid helper in chromium-sandbox is deliberately absent: one setuid-root binary less on the image.
chromium
# fonts-dejavu-core: the fallback font. The UI ships its own typeface, but text it does not cover (and the browser's
# own error pages) needs one system font, and fontconfig refuses to render without any. fontconfig-config already
# pulls it in as a dependency; it is named here so that a dependency change cannot drop it unnoticed.
fonts-dejavu-core
# libpam-systemd: pam_systemd, which pai-kiosk.service's PAMName=login uses to register the kiosk as a login session
# on seat0 (so logind can hand cage the display and input devices) and to create /run/user/<uid>. Also a dependency
# of other packages here; named for the same reason as the font.
libpam-systemd

# Installer (os/installer, installer/, docs/architecture/INSTALLER_DESIGN.md): the live system installs itself, so the
# tools that partition, format, copy the running root and install the boot loader are part of the image. Installed with
# --apt-recommends false; measured in debian:trixie-slim on top of systemd, udev, cryptsetup and live-boot: these eight
# packages and their hard dependencies (libfdisk1, libext2fs2t64, libefivar1t64, libefiboot1t64, libfreetype6, libfuse3-4,
# libpng16-16t64, libbrotli1, gettext-base, ...) are 27 packages, about 11 MB to download and 42 MiB installed, mostly
# GRUB's modules. util-linux (lsblk, wipefs, blkid, mount), kmod, udev and cryptsetup are already part of the image.
# os-prober is deliberately absent (grub-common only recommends it): it mounts every other disk to look for operating
# systems, which an installer that touches exactly one named disk must never do. tests/security/test_os_hardening.py
# forbids it.
# fdisk: sfdisk, which writes and verifies the GPT partition table from a script (the "fdisk" package carries it since
# util-linux 2.38; it is not in the base).
fdisk
# dosfstools: mkfs.vfat for the EFI system partition.
dosfstools
# e2fsprogs: mkfs.ext4 for /boot and the root filesystem, and tune2fs/e2fsck. Priority required, so debootstrap may
# already install it; named here so that a change to the base cannot drop it unnoticed (as the font and libpam-systemd).
e2fsprogs
# rsync: copies the running root filesystem to the target preserving ownership, modes, hard links, ACLs and xattrs,
# staying on one filesystem.
rsync
# grub-common, grub2-common: grub-install, grub-mkconfig, grub-probe and update-grub, the boot loader tools the engine
# runs inside the target.
grub-common
grub2-common
# grub-pc-bin: the i386-pc modules for `grub-install --target=i386-pc` (BIOS, GPT with a BIOS boot partition).
grub-pc-bin
# grub-efi-amd64-bin: the x86_64-efi modules for `grub-install --target=x86_64-efi --removable --no-nvram` (UEFI; also
# pulls grub-efi-amd64-unsigned). No signed shim or efibootmgr: Secure Boot and firmware variables are out of scope.
grub-efi-amd64-bin
